  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nMorning light rain associated with a warm front/isentropic lift is \ndeparting the region, with breaking clouds across eastern Ohio and \nfar northwest PA. This will set the stage for some destabilization \nduring the afternoon hours ahead of expected evening convection.\n\nA mid-level shortwave trough is still projected to cross Michigan \nand the eastern Great Lakes this afternoon and evening, propelling a \nsurface cold front across the Upper Ohio Valley tonight. \nThunderstorms are still forecast to develop ahead of the boundary \nthis afternoon as it approaches Lake Erie, with a fairly solid line \nof convection depicted by most CAMs crossing the region this \nevening. Shear is not in question, with 35-45 knots of 0-6km bulk \nshear during the afternoon and evening. Also, HREF 25th/75th \npercentile SBCAPE values peak in the 600-1100 J/kg range this \nafternoon.\n\nThe trick of the tail remains convective timing. There are still \nsome variances amongst the various HREF members. Looking at FKL in \nparticular, the FV3 brings the line through by 00Z, while the 12Z \nHREF waits until around 02Z, and the 3k NAM until 03Z. The overall \ntrend does appear a bit slower overall. Thus, buoyancy should \nalready be dropping off from the peak levels noted above by the time \nconvection arrives in the hours leading up to sunset. Thus suggests \na slight reduction in the potential for strong to severe \nstorms. Still, with sufficient shear throughout and some \nlingering instability, an at least semi-organized line of \nconvection is likely to impact locations mainly north of \nPittsburgh through the late evening. Despite the shear, hail \npotential may be a bit lower than previous given the expected \ntrend toward linear organization (as well as marginal mid-level \nlapse rates), but a modest wind threat still is on the table to \nthe north and west of Pittsburgh. The window for potential \nsevere storms still appears rather short, say 2-3 hours, before \nstorms decay to sub- severe levels as they sink south towards \neast-central OH/far southwest PA. Rapid weakening should then \ncontinue, with the line nearly totally dissipating as a few \nshowers along the I-70 corridor overnight.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nEnsembles remain in good agreement that large-scale \ntroughing remains in place over the Great Lakes/northeast CONUS\nthrough at least the end of the next work week, with near to \nbelow- normal temperatures throughout. A few shortwaves move \nthrough the larger trough through the period, providing shower \nchances. Minor strength/timing differences remain in the various\nsolutions.\n\nOne such wave completes passage on Monday, with surface high \npressure building in its wake Monday night, along with 850mb \ntemperatures dipping below zero. There is fairly high confidence in \nclear and calm conditions as the high center moves overhead. This \nwill represent our next (potentially last until fall?) chance at \nfrost/freeze conditions. NBM probabilities of minimum temperatures \n36 or below are 50 percent or greater to the north and east of \nPittsburgh, as well as in valley locations in the terrain. Headlines \nmay eventually be needed.",
